    "message": {
     "content": "### 1. Is this a good offer?\n\n**Yes, this is an exceptionally strong offer for a Google Senior Product Manager (L6).**\n\n* **Market Benchmark:** The median total compensation (TC) for an L6 PM in the Bay Area is typically around **$500,000\u2013$530,000**. A package with $1.34M in equity and a $200k sign-on puts you in the **top quartile / top decile** of the L6 band.\n* **Base Salary:** At **$260,000**, your base is near the top of the posted $271,000 range (approx. 96th percentile).\n* **Year 1 vs. Ongoing Reality:**\n  * **Meta (Current):** ~$526,000/yr (with ~$200k equity vesting in the next 12 months).\n  * **Google 4-Year Average (Recurring):** $260k base + $52k bonus (20%) + $335k/yr equity = **~$647,000/yr** (a **+$121,000/yr or ~23% increase** over Meta).\n  * **Google Year 1 (Cash + Equity):** Google typically uses a front-loaded vesting schedule (e.g., 33% / 33% / 22% / 12% or 38% / 32% / 20% / 10%). Assuming standard 33% Y1 vesting + $200k sign-on:\n    * Base: $260k\n    * Bonus: $52k\n    * Stock (33% of $1.34M): ~$442k\n    * Sign-on: $200k\n    * **Year 1 Total:** **~$954,000** (nearly double your current Meta Y1 cash flow).\n\n---\n\n### 2. Should you accept, negotiate, or walk away?\n\n**You should negotiate first.** \n\n* **Do not walk away:** The financial uplift is substantial over a 4-year horizon (an extra ~$500k+ in cumulative comp plus immediate liquidity).\n* **Do not sign without countering:** You were sourced passively, you are performing well at Meta, and you have no urgent reason to leave. This gives you maximum leverage.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Understanding the Equity & \"Vesting Cliff\"\n\nGoogle uses front-loaded equity to make initial years lucrative:\n* **Years 1 & 2:** You vest ~33% per year (~$442k/yr).\n* **Year 3:** Drops to 22% (~$295k).\n* **Year 4:** Drops to 12% (~$161k).\n\nThe recruiter is correct that **refreshers stack**: Google grants annual refreshers (which vest incrementally over subsequent years) specifically to bridge the drop-off in Years 3 and 4. While performance-dependent, standard performance at L6 yields refresh grants that cushion this drop.\n\n---\n\n### 4. What to ask for and how to negotiate\n\nBecause your base is already near the band ceiling ($271k) and your sign-on is healthy ($200k), your biggest levers are **Equity (RSUs)** and **Year 2 Sign-on protection**.\n\n#### Recommended Target Counters:\n1. **Equity (Primary Lever):** Ask for **$1.5M \u2013 $1.6M** in RSUs over 4 years (up from $1.34M).\n2. **Base Salary (Minor Lever):** Ask to max the band at **$270,000** (up from $260,000).\n3. **Sign-on Structure (Alternative Lever):** If they push back on equity, ask to convert the sign-on into a two-year structure (e.g., **$150k in Year 1 + $100k in Year 2**).\n\n---\n\n### 5. Negotiation Script / Approach\n\nContact your recruiter via phone or email and frame the conversation around your satisfaction at Meta and your target number to make the move:\n\n> *\"Thank you for putting this offer together. I was really impressed by the team and the scope of the role during the interviews.*\n>\n> *As you know, I wasn't actively looking when Google reached out. I'm in a great position at Meta as an IC6 with strong momentum and substantial unvested equity. Because compensation and long-term trajectory are major priorities for me, I want to make sure the gap justifies leaving my current scope.*\n>\n> *If we can get the equity grant to **$1.55M** and adjust the base salary to the top of the posted band at **$270,000**, I would be thrilled to sign and decline other discussions immediately.\"*\n\n### Key Advice:\n* **Don't bluff about fake offers:** Ground your leverage entirely in your current Meta trajectory, IC6 level, and the opportunity cost of walking away.\n* **Confirm the vesting schedule:** Ask the recruiter explicitly whether this offer follows the standard front-loaded schedule (33/33/22/12) so you can model your tax and cash flow accurately for Years 1\u20134.",
